2. **full_measure**: ambient = data space with a specified Borel measure μ (e.g., Gaussian measure on a Sobolev space, or finite-dimensional approximation); generic set = measurable set with μ(D \ C) = 0 (or μ(C)=1 for probability measures); excluded = μ-null. Quantifier: "∃ C measurable, μ(C)=1: ∀ d ∈ C, P(d)". Requires a measure class specified (different measures → different generic sets). Note: no canonical measure on the full nonlinear constraint manifold; hence "measure class" must be declared.
3. **open_dense_escape**: generic set = an open dense set (not merely dense G_δ); quantifier: "∃ U open dense ⊆ D: ∀ d ∈ U, P(d)". This is stronger than comeager (open dense ⊆ comeager) but weaker than "residual" in the sense of allowing a meager exceptional set that is closed nowhere dense. Actually open dense is a special case of comeager. Transfer: open_dense_escape ⇒ residual_comeager holds (if the space is Baire? No: open dense sets are comeager by definition — countable intersection containing one open dense set; no Baire needed for "comeager" definition though the useful property of nonemptiness needs Baire). Conversely comeager ⇒ open dense fails in general (e.g., R \ Q is comeager but not open dense).
